\description{
Evaluates prediction accuracy for any fitted \code{\link{mysterycall_lmm}},
\code{\link{mysterycall_poisson_model}}, or \code{\link{mysterycall_nb_model}} object by
comparing actual outcome values against in-sample or out-of-sample
predictions. Optionally back-transforms predictions from a log scale and
produces an actual-vs-predicted scatter plot with a 45-degree reference line.
}
\details{
\strong{When to use:}
After fitting a model with \code{\link[=mysterycall_lmm]{mysterycall_lmm()}}, \code{\link[=mysterycall_poisson_model]{mysterycall_poisson_model()}},
or \code{\link[=mysterycall_nb_model]{mysterycall_nb_model()}}, call this function to quantify how closely the
fitted values track the observed outcomes. Useful for model comparison and
for reporting prediction accuracy in the methods section.

\strong{Back-transformation for log-transformed LMM:}
When the LMM was fitted on a \code{log1p()}-transformed outcome
(\code{fit$log_transformed = TRUE}), \code{back_transform} is automatically set to
\code{TRUE}. Predictions from \code{predict()} are on the log scale; \code{exp()} is
applied before computing errors, so MAE and RMSE are reported in the units
of the original outcome (days). Actual values stored in the model frame are
also on the log scale and receive the same \code{exp()} back-transform for a
consistent comparison.
}
\section{Metrics}{

\describe{
\item{MAE}{\verb{mean(|actual - predicted|)}. Scale-dependent; same units as
the outcome.}
\item{RMSE}{\code{sqrt(mean((actual - predicted)^2))}. More sensitive to large
errors than MAE.}
\item{R2 (Pearson)}{\code{cor(actual, predicted)^2}. Measures linear agreement;
does \strong{not} equal the model's marginal R^2.}
\item{MAPE}{\verb{mean(|actual - predicted| / |actual|) * 100}. Unit-free
percentage; undefined when actual = 0.}
}
}

\section{Out-of-sample use}{

Pass \code{newdata} for external validation. The grouping variable (random
intercept) should be present in \code{newdata} so that \code{lme4} / \pkg{glmmTMB}
can condition on estimated BLUPs; pass it as \code{re.form = NA} (via
\code{predict()} directly) for pure population-level predictions.
}

